-Change Data Capture is wired through SQLite's preupdate and commit hooks. The hooks are registered on the single `rwDB` connection — the same one that does all writes — via `RegisterPreUpdateHook` and `RegisterCommitHook`. Hook installation goes through `conn.Raw` because the registration methods are on the SQLite-specific connection type, not on the abstract `database/sql` connection.
+Change Data Capture is wired through SQLite's preupdate and commit hooks. The hooks are registered on the single `rwDB` connection — the same one that does all writes — via `RegisterPreUpdateHook` and `RegisterCommitHook`. Hook installation goes through `conn.Raw` because the registration methods are on the SQLite-specific connection type, not on the abstract `database/sql` connection. `RegisterPreCommitTxHook` and `RegisterPreStatementTxHook` are a third, unrelated kind of hook: plain Go callbacks `executeWithConn`/`RequestWithContext` invoke directly, with no SQLite callback and no `conn.Raw` involved at all — see below.
 
-`CDCStreamer` (in `cdc.go`) is the in-package hook target. The preupdate hook appends an event to a pending group; the commit hook stamps the group with the current time, looks up column names per table (cached for the duration of the group), and non-blockingly sends the group to a channel. If the channel is full the event is dropped and a stat increments — by design, CDC is at-least-once on the receiver side, not back-pressuring the writer. The `CDCStreamer` lives in `db/` rather than in `cdc/` because its hook installation, its per-statement reset, and its lifecycle are all driven from inside the database write path; it is tightly bound to the SQLite hook plumbing and would only become harder to reason about if moved further away.
+`CDCStreamer` (in `cdc.go`) is the in-package hook target. The preupdate hook appends an event to a pending group; the commit hook stamps the group with the current time, looks up column names per table, and non-blockingly sends the group to a channel. If the channel is full the event is dropped and a stat increments — by design, CDC is at-least-once on the receiver side, not back-pressuring the writer. The `CDCStreamer` lives in `db/` rather than in `cdc/` because its hook installation, its per-statement reset, and its lifecycle are all driven from inside the database write path; it is tightly bound to the SQLite hook plumbing and would only become harder to reason about if moved further away.
+
+Per-table column names (which columns to exclude from a table's before/after row data is a separate, optional `column_filter`) are cached *across* commits, not just for the duration of one group — recomputing them from `db` on every commit is real overhead under sustained writes. A schema-version counter is the staleness signal: the commit hook re-reads it once per commit, and any change (or a read failure) discards the cache and re-derives it fresh. A database restore and a Raft leadership change can each replace what "the database" means without necessarily changing that counter observably, so `CDCStreamer.InvalidateAll()` is called explicitly for those two cases instead.
+
+That counter and those column names normally come from `ColumnsNameProvider.SchemaVersion()`/`ColumnNames()`, read through a connection distinct from the writer's (a pooled, read-only one) -- the same source and cache for every table, whether or not it has a `column_filter`. That's correct whenever a DDL statement against a table belongs to some earlier, already-completed commit -- the ordinary case, since `CDCStreamer`'s caller resets its pending events at the start of every new transaction -- but not when a single `Request` explicitly wraps a DDL statement and a row change to that table in one transaction (`Request.Transaction`, executed as one `BeginTx`/`Commit` pair in `db.go`'s `executeWithConn`/`RequestWithContext`): a separate, pooled connection cannot observe that transaction until it commits, and the commit hook itself cannot safely query anything, even read-only, through the connection that invoked it -- that restriction is exactly what an earlier version of this feature got wrong, retaining and reusing a raw connection from inside the hooks themselves.
+
+`CDCStreamer.PreCommitRefresh` covers the same-transaction case instead, from a different point in the write path entirely: `db.RegisterPreCommitTxHook` installs a callback that `executeWithConn`/`RequestWithContext` invoke as ordinary, sequential Go code, through the transaction's own `*sql.Tx`, immediately before calling `tx.Commit()` -- not from inside any SQLite callback, so none of the restrictions on hook reentrancy or `Conn.Raw` connection lifetime apply. By that point every statement in the transaction, DDL included, has already run against that same `*sql.Tx`, so a read through it sees the transaction's own uncommitted effects the way only the connection driving it can. `PreCommitRefresh` uses this to rebuild the cache for every table the pending batch needs, live, and marks `CDCStreamer.liveRefreshed` so the real, synchronous SQLite commit hook that fires moments later trusts that cache as-is rather than re-validating it against the pooled connection's still-pre-commit view. A table `PreCommitRefresh` cannot confirm (a query failure, never escalated to fail the write itself) is not left to the pooled path's fallback either, since that could describe the wrong schema for exactly the transaction this exists to handle -- its pending events are marked with an error instead.
+
+Knowing the transaction's final columns is only half of it, because a row's captured values are positional in whatever columns the table had *when that row was read*, and a later statement in the same transaction can add, drop, or rename one. Reconciling those values against the final columns by position is what would let a dropped column's leftover value be published under whatever column ends up in its old slot -- an excluded column's value included, since equal widths make the substitution invisible to any length check. `RegisterPreStatementTxHook` closes that gap: it installs a callback the same two functions invoke, through the same `*sql.Tx`, before each statement of an explicit transaction. `CDCStreamer.SnapshotCaptureLayouts` uses it to pin the columns of any already-captured row before the next statement can change them, so each pending event carries the layout it was actually read under. The commit hook then matches values to final columns by column *identity* (`remapCDCRow`): a surviving column keeps its value wherever it now sits, a column that did not exist at capture time is an explicit NULL, and a dropped or excluded column is simply never selected. An event no statement followed needs no snapshot -- nothing could have changed the table after it was read -- so the final columns describe it exactly.
 
 The Store owns the streamer's lifecycle but delegates the row-data conversion (`normalizeCDCValues`) to this package, which is also where the SQLite-to-rqlite type mapping for query results lives.
 
